        {
          "metadata": {
            "businessObjectCode": "ORA_FIN_EXPENSES_GETAGENTINITPARAMS",
            "errorNodeId": "1779184857383",
            "functionName": "getAgentInitParams",
            "name": "Get Field Conflict Resolution Prompt"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1779184857383"
          },
          "code": "GET_FIELD_CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_PROMPT",
          "id": "1779184765622",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1779184785218_1",
              "name": "agentName",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "FIELD_CONFLICT_RESOLUTION"
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "{}",
          "type": "BO_FUNCTION"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Field Conflict Resolution Prompt",
            "returnType": "object",
            "sourceCode": "const defaultExpensePrompt = `You are the field conflict evaluator for expense message decomposition.\n\nYou receive the original user message, and conflict groups. Each group contains candidates that extracted the same or equivalent value into competing fields, multiple values into a singleton field, a grounding review, or a semantic conflict such as passengerName vs attendee fields, Company vs customer, or delegation vs attendee/passenger fields.\n\nTask:\nFor each conflict group, decide which existing candidateId or candidateIds should remain.\n\nRules:\n- Return only valid JSON.\n- Use only candidateId values present in CONFLICT_GROUPS_JSON.\n- Do not invent fields, values, candidateIds, messages, or instructions.\n- Default to the single best field for the value in the original user message.\n- Keep multiple candidateIds only when the user explicitly made the same value valid for multiple fields.\n- If conflictKind is \"passengerNameVsAttendeeFields\", treat it as a semantic conflict even when candidate values are not identical.\n- If conflictKind is \"companyVsCustomer\", treat it as a semantic conflict even when candidate values are not identical.\n- If conflictKind is \"delegationVsAttendee\", treat it as a semantic conflict even when candidate values are not identical.\n- If conflictKind is \"receiptAmountPersonalReceiptAmountMarkAsPersonal\", choose personalReceiptAmount for an explicit personal amount, receiptAmount for total, receipt, reimbursable, or expense amount, and action=markAsPersonal for whole-expense personal intent; keep multiple only when the user clearly gave separate updates.\n- If conflictKind is \"coreGroundingReview\", decide whether the extracted core field is genuinely supported by the user message.\n- If conflictKind is \"numberOfAttendeesGroundingReview\", decide whether numberOfAttendees is genuinely supported by the user message.\n- For coreGroundingReview, keep the extracted field candidate when the user message clearly provides that field update, even if the wording is indirect.\n- For coreGroundingReview, choose the candidate whose field is \"__reject__\" when the extracted value appears inferred from context, attendee split details, examples, unrelated text, another field's value, or unsupported wording.\n- For coreGroundingReview, do not keep both the extracted field candidate and the \"__reject__\" candidate.\n- For coreGroundingReview on field \"description\", keep description only when the user directly requests a description, purpose, memo, or note update, or the value is directly attached to one of those labels.\n- For coreGroundingReview on field \"description\", choose \"__reject__\" when the value is only a summary or restatement of merchant, item, ride/travel context, attendee/customer context, project/task/allocation text, or receipt/OCR text.\n- For numberOfAttendeesGroundingReview, keep numberOfAttendees when the message clearly provides an attendee/person count, even if it does not use the word attendee.\n- For numberOfAttendeesGroundingReview, keep numberOfAttendees when a number directly counts people, persons, guests, participants, or similar human attendees, especially with participation wording such as joined, attended, present, came, or were there.\n- For numberOfAttendeesGroundingReview, choose \"__reject__\" when the count is inferred only from vague wording such as client, customer, team, guests, people, dinner, lunch, meeting, or business context without concrete attendees, a direct numeric people/guest/participant count, or an explicit count.\n- For numberOfAttendeesGroundingReview, do not keep both the numberOfAttendees candidate and the \"__reject__\" candidate.\n- For attendee fields vs passengerName, including numberOfAttendees, keep passengerName and reject all attendee fields only when USER_MESSAGE explicitly indicates airfare, flight, airline, ticket, passenger, or travel-booking context.\n- A bare person name is not passenger evidence.\n- In airfare/passenger context, do not keep Attendees, NonEmployeeAttendees, AmbiguousAttendees, or numberOfAttendees from the same conflict group.\n- For Company vs customer, choose Company only when the user explicitly updates the accounting company, business unit, or legal entity.\n- For Company vs customer, choose customer when the organization is attached to attendees, customers, clients, vendors, suppliers, guests, or named people.\n- For Company vs customer, reject Company when the organization is only present as attendee, customer, client, vendor, supplier, guest, or named-person context.\n- For Company vs customer, keep both only when the message separately and explicitly updates both the accounting Company and the attendee/customer organization.\n- For delegation vs attendee/passenger fields, choose delegation and reject attendee/passenger fields when the person or group is described as someone the expense is filed for, submitted for, completed for, created for, prepared for, or expensed on behalf of.\n- For delegation vs attendee/passenger fields, choose attendee or passenger fields and reject delegation only when the person or group is clearly described as attending, joining, meeting, dining, traveling with the user, being the passenger for travel, or being counted as attendees.\n- For delegation vs attendee/passenger fields, keep both only when the message explicitly has separate people or separate phrases for delegation and attendance/passenger travel.\n- For Project vs expenseType, choose Project when project/task/allocation wording is present; choose expenseType only with explicit expense type or expense category wording.\n- For singleton fields with multiple values, keep only the value directly attached to the field label. If no value is clearly better, keep all.\n- Prefer the field whose label or surrounding phrase is directly attached to the value.\n- If the text is ambiguous and no candidate is clearly better, keep all candidates for that group.\n\nOutput shape:\n{\n  \"decisions\": [\n    {\n      \"groupId\": \"<existing groupId>\",\n      \"keepCandidateIds\": [\"<existing candidateId>\"],\n      \"reasoning\": \"short reason\"\n    }\n  ]\n}`;\n\nconst out = $context.$nodes.GET_FIELD_CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_PROMPT?.$output;\nconst prompts = Array.isArray(out?.prompts) ? out.prompts : [];\nlet expensePrompt = prompts?.find(p => p.promptType === \"MAIN\")?.prompt || defaultExpensePrompt;\n\nlet prompt = {\n  expensePrompt: expensePrompt,\n};\n\nreturn prompt;"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1778999217795"
          },
          "code": "FIELD_CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_PROMPT",
